KW  - Autoencoder
KW  - Early warning
AB  - Centrifugal pumps operating under gas-liquid two-phase flow conditions are highly susceptible to performance collapse due to surging, characterized by catastrophic drops in head, efficiency, and flow rate. Traditional surging prediction methods—relying on experimental correlations, computational fluid dynamics, or threshold-based monitoring—exhibit significant limitations in accuracy, real-time capability, and generalizability. To address this gap, this study proposes an intelligent early warning framework integrating a One-dimensional Convolutional Neural Network-Bidirectional Gated Recurrent Unit (1DCNN-BiGRU) autoencoder with contrastive learning. Pressure pulsation data were acquired from a transparent centrifugal pump across varied inlet gas volume fractions, rotational speeds, and seven monitoring positions. The baseline autoencoder, trained exclusively on non-surging data and optimized via Bayesian hyperparameter tuning, achieved effective reconstruction of normal operation patterns (max error <3 %). To enhance sensitivity near instability thresholds, a contrastive learning strategy was introduced: encoder weights were frozen, limited surging samples were incorporated, and a novel contrastive loss function with a dynamic threshold ε was designed to cluster normal samples while distancing surging representations. Results demonstrate that the contrastive model significantly outperformed the baseline, reducing the missed detection rate (MDR) by 17.3 % on average across all sensor locations. Optimal warning performance occurred at the volute tongue (Position P3), achieving an F1-score of 0.937, false alarm rate (FAR) of 5.8 %, MDR of 6.8 %, and response time of 0.347 s. This work establishes a new paradigm for real-time surging prognostics, with direct implications for reliability in energy, petrochemical, and water infrastructure systems.
