AB  - Individual spatiotemporal activity data serve as a critical foundation for constructing high-fidelity urban digital twins, yet its acquisition and application face challenges such as scarcity, skewed distribution, and privacy constraints. Existing models focus on single aspects: either population synthesis or activity generation, neglecting the spatiotemporal behavioral diversity arising from heterogeneous personal characteristics and household structures. To address this, we propose a novel deep generative model to capture the complex dependencies among individual attributes, activities, and locations. First, generated a synthetic attribute dataset incorporating personal characteristics and household structures. Next, we employed variational embedding to encode activity location sequences into a discrete latent space, capturing the temporal evolution of latent distributions. Finally, we leveraged correlations between attributes and latent variables for conditional generation, that integrating individual attributes with spatiotemporal activity sequences. Experimental results show that our model produced realistic, privacy-preserving synthetic datasets that maintained the statistical distributions of real-world mobility spatiotemporal patterns. These diverse individual spatiotemporal activity data have been successfully applied in urban digital twins, providing reliable data support for applications in urban planning, smart cities, and public health.
