AB  - As personalized services become ubiquitous, the fairness of recommendation systems has drawn significant interest, graph neural network-based adversarial debiasing methods have also made remarkable strides in the realm of recommendation systems. However, many of these approaches neglect the issue of intersectional two-sided fairness, which is intricately linked to the exposure of sensitive information within embeddings. Furthermore, the graph aggregation operations can exacerbate this risk, potentially escalating the level of unfairness in the recommendations. To tackle these issues, we introduce a bifurcated adversarial network with feature modulation, which quantifies and corrects biases from sensitive information in aggregated embeddings. Specifically, we initially implement a feature modulation bias capture mechanism to quantify the influence of various sensitive information within the aggregated embeddings, converting it into feature impact factors. These factors are subsequently integrated into a bifurcated adversarial network to generate intersectional group factors, which allows us to dynamically adjust the intensity with which the filters operate on different groups. Lastly, we develop a method for weighted feature fusion to derive the final debiased embeddings. Experiments on two datasets show that our model significantly reduces intersectional two-sided unfairness and outperforms the state-of-the-art models.
