AB  - This study suggests a new method of improving seismic resiliency of Cross-Laminated Timber (CLT) buildings through the use of machine learning (ML) algorithms to create resilient fragility curves. This study uses a database of various strip analysis structural responses of CLT buildings and presents an integrated engineering analysis with ML based on estimating the seismic performance of the buildings for levels of earthquake intensity. The methods are based on an analytical study of wall segments that have windows and enable models to investigate wall segment impacts on drift limits. Design of the sample structures was based upon the ASCE 7–16 provisions and, to develop cumulative probability distribution functions of inter-story drifts, dynamic analysis using time-history was conducted. In the present study, four probabilistic classifier models (i.e., probabilistic support vector machine (PSVM), Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBOOST), Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LightGBM), and probabilistic K-nearest neighbors (PKNN) classifiers) were utilized. A full assessment of model performance was achieved with the LightGBM model, which resulted in 91 % accuracy and an F1 score of 0.93 for critical structural response prediction. When compared, the ML fragility curves demonstrated excellent agreement with fragility curves from a more traditional multiple strip analysis, with a difference of <0.05 under probabilistic thresholds or a structural failure. Overall, this method demonstrates the ability of ML to predict the strength of CLT structures for structural engineering, while also assisting geotechnical engineers and others to have more faith in their seismic risk estimation of CLT structures. This new coupling has excellent potential to improve the safety and design of resilient building materials in earthquake prone regions to support a sustainable resilient architectural future for urban communities.
