AB  - Solubility Named Entity Recognition (SNER) is a critical step in constructing solubility knowledge graphs from unstructured texts, yet it remains challenging due to the complex nomenclature of chemical entities and the scarcity of annotated data. To address these challenges, this paper proposes MetaSol, a novel ensemble learning framework for few-shot SNER. The core contributions of our work are threefold: Firstly, a dual-feature encoding scheme that dynamically fuses character-level and word-level embeddings is introduced, leveraging SCIBERT for robust feature representation. Secondly, our work presents an enhanced Model-Agnostic Meta-Learning (MAML) algorithm, incorporating a self-attention-based loss weighting strategy and adaptive inner-loop learning rates to improve optimization stability and generalization. Finally, the model effectively integrates convolutional neural networks (CNN) for local feature extraction and a conditional random field (CRF) layer for structured decoding. Extensive experiments on the ChEMU-2020 dataset demonstrate that MetaSol achieves a state-of-the-art F1-score of 0.9188, significantly outperforming strong baseline models. This study provides an effective solution for SNER and offers valuable insights for few-shot NER tasks in related scientific domains.
