AB  - Modeling and simulation of high power microwave (HPM) breakdown, a multiscale and multiphysics phenomenon, is computationally intensive as it requires solving Maxwell’s equations coupled with a plasma continuity equation. We present a hybrid physics-data framework for simulation of HPM breakdown, wherein the computationally expensive electromagnetic field updates from the finite-difference time-domain (FDTD) solver are replaced by a Fourier Neural Operator (FNO) surrogate trained to predict the cycle-averaged scattered electric field. The continuity equation governing diffusion–ionization dynamics is solved using a physics-based C solver, while the FNO surrogate is implemented in Python and coupled through a controller, enabling reuse of legacy solvers with minimal overhead. The FNO has been trained on datasets obtained from high fidelity FDTD-plasma simulations of microwave streamer evolution (using 512 × 256 grid) and achieved strong predictive accuracy (mean square error ∼0.04, average percent error ∼0.12, structural similarity index ∼0.9999). The FNO-based hybrid framework accurately reproduces plasma morphology, field distributions, and streamer growth dynamics, showing near-perfect agreement with FDTD-fluid results. The framework provides nearly a ∼60× speedup, reducing multi-day simulations to under an hour. A comparative analysis with a U-shaped encoder–decoder convolutional neural network surrogate indicates that the FNO provides substantially higher, near-deterministic accuracy at each iteration, which is essential in the hybrid framework to prevent error accumulation over successive steps. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first demonstration of a hybrid data-driven and physics-based framework for plasma fluid modeling, offering a scalable and generalizable paradigm for accelerating plasma simulations.
