AB  - This work investigates the complex unsteady flow velocity, temperature, and concentration profiles of the Casson hybrid nanofluid, moving through a vertical rotating cone. The effect of thermal radiation, variable magnetic field and the chemical reaction are also considered in this study. The dimensional governing equations, which include continuity, momentum, energy and concentration equations as well as the imposed boundary conditions, are transformed into non-dimensional form using applicable similarity transformation. The numerical method is used to solve the associated system of equations. The study shows the shear-stress rates as well as thermal and mass transfer rates, using the Runge-Kutta Fehlberg approach to solve strongly non-linear problems. The effect of the pertinent parameters is shown and examined using tables and graph. By combining nanoparticles of copper, molybdenum disulfide with kerosene base fluid, we have successfully created versatile and multipurpose hybrid nanofluid. The novelty of the present research lies in its comprehensive comparison of two distinct fluid flow scenarios, Casson nanofluid and Casson hybrid nanofluid, focusing specifically on their respective skin-friction coefficients and heat-transfer rates. This fluid numerous cutting-edge uses such as coating, energy storage, purifying water, improved heat exchangers, etc. There has been comparison with prior published studies, and the findings show great agreement. The results show that the tangential velocity increases with the higher values of the unsteady parameter, slip-velocity parameter and magnetic field parameter. Various scenarios are set for training, testing, and validation in order to obtain the model's estimated output. Regression, histogram, fitness curve, and mean square error are used in the analysis. The remarkable accuracy of the developed artificial neural network model during the training, validation, and testing phases demonstrates its dependability.
