AB  - In this study, we present a new hybrid approach, Multi-Agent Proximal Policy Optimization (MAPPO)-supported dynamic behavior tree evolution, for improving decentralized decision-making in robotic multi-agent systems. Behavior Trees, despite providing modular and interpretable control structures, are inherently static and may not be able to adapt to dynamic environments. Our method provides scalable and interpretable decision making by allowing agents to independently update their Behavior Tree structures using policy gradients acquired from centralized MAPPO training. We assess the framework within Robot Operating System and Gazebo in three multi-agent scenarios (task allocation, obstacle avoidance, and collaborative execution), and show the superior performance in terms of task success rate, decision latency, structural complexity, adaptation rate, and coordination efficiency with respect to baseline methods. The experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method appropriately strikes a trade-off between the learning-based adaptability and the interpretability of the behavior, which is a hopeful method in the real world multi-agent robotic applications.
