AB  - This study addressed the reduction in accuracy for small-object detection caused by size variation and occlusion in artificial intelligence–based computer vision. We built Unreal Engine 4 scenes with three-dimensional models of seven target types and collected 1521 images using an AirSim-based unmanned aerial vehicle simulator at a 60° pitch and 45° field of view, capturing paired high- and low-resolution images. The proposed Z-shaped cropping algorithm generated occlusion samples through a cut-and-replace strategy, improving detection confidence by 0.13, 0.15, and 0.23 under 20 %, 30 %, and 40 % occlusion, respectively. Optimal performance intervals of the attention and feature enhancement modules were theoretically proved and experimentally verified. Consequently, an optimized You Only Look Once Version 11 model was developed. The model with an hourglass-ghost block and an area attention with C2f module achieved gains of 5.31 % in mean average precision at intersection over union of 0.50 (mAP@0.50) and 5.42 % in mean average precision at 0.50–0.95 (mAP@0.50:0.95) compared with the baseline. Results revealed an efficient artificial intelligence–based solution for detecting small, partially occluded targets in complex scenes.
