AB  - Remaining useful life (RUL) prediction is assuming an increasingly critical role in health monitoring and maintenance of mechanical systems. Considering the data privacy constraints and data distribution discrepancy in multi-client distributed collaborative training, how to boost the RUL prediction accuracy under cross-domain conditions is still a critical issue worth studying. Hence, this paper proposes a federated learning method with personalized dynamic aggregation and local-adversarial for RUL prediction of rolling bearings. Firstly, a parallel feature cross-attention fusion network incorporating multi-scale convolution and gated recurrent units is constructed to effectively extract spatio-temporal features from time-series data, and then to accomplish the RUL prediction. Secondly, considering the difference of performance and data of different clients, a dynamic model aggregation strategy guided by client validation loss is proposed to generate a global model on the server, so as to improve its perception of multi-condition data. Concurrently, a personalized local-adversarial weighted fusion strategy is designed on the client to integrate the global model with the client model effectively, thereby improving the personalization and generalization of the client model. Two sets of experimental data are utilized to analyze and validate the proposed methodology. The findings demonstrate that the proposed approach is capable of enhancing the RUL prediction accuracy of rolling bearings across operating conditions, and offers an effective technical support for the predictive maintenance of industrial equipment.
