AB  - In highly complex and dynamically changing manufacturing environments, the new job arrivals represent a common and unpredictable disturbance. This often renders existing scheduling plans ineffective, thereby compromising production stability. However, many existing dynamic scheduling models incorporate setup and transportation times into processing time or assume unlimited transport resources. This approach overlooks the coordination between automated guided vehicles and cranes, as well as quantity constraints. To address this challenge, this paper focuses on an energy-efficient dynamic flexible job shop scheduling problem with limited transportation resources (EDFJSP-T) under new job arrivals. We construct a more realistic dynamic scheduling environment by incorporating factors such as variable machine speeds and double-deck gantry cranes. First, a mixed-integer linear programming model is formulated, with the objective of minimizing the makespan, total energy consumption, workload of critical machines, and instability. Second, a solution framework based on a complete rescheduling strategy is developed, and a hybrid memetic algorithm driven by reinforcement learning (HRLMA) is proposed. The algorithm employs a novel two-phase heuristic decoding scheme to efficiently solve the rescheduling model, designs an improved dual-parameter adaptive learning strategy to dynamically regulate the crossover and mutation probabilities, and integrates an objective-guided variable neighborhood search framework based on tabu search to enhance the search efficiency. Comparative experiments are performed across 20 static and dynamic scheduling cases to verify the effectiveness of the proposed algorithm. The results indicate the superiority of HRLMA in solving the EDFJSP-T.
