AB  - The increasing frequency of extreme events and the growing penetration of intermittent photovoltaics (PV) highlight the urgent need for planning strategies that enhance distribution network resilience while considering the economic operation. To address the issue, this paper develops a unified framework for planning shared energy storage (SES) in distribution networks that seamlessly integrates scenario generation, resilience assessment, and investment-operation optimization under uncertainty. First, an attention recurrent adversarial autoencoder (AR-AAE) is designed to augment the foundational scenarios required for downstream SES planning and resilience assessment. Second, an “attack-defense” resilience assessment method is proposed by casting network disturbances as adversarial moves on a graph and training a graph-attention deep-reinforcement learning (GAT-DRL) agent in a self-healing distribution network simulator. The agent plays the attacker and its average rewards directly quantify resilience of the distribution network supported by energy storage. Third, considering the participation of SES in distribution network auxiliary services and the resilience assessment index, an AAE-assisted game distributionally robust optimization (DRO) model for SES capacity is established based on multi-stakeholder collaborative operation (e.g., PV prosumers). The model optimizes the SES investment costs and operation benefits, subject to the resilience-driven chance constraint. Finally, case studies demonstrate that the SES planning framework can effectively enhance distribution network resilience while improving the cost-benefit trade-off in multi-stakeholder collaborative operation.
