KW  - Inter-domain similarity
KW  - Soft threshold denoising
KW  - Few shot learning
AB  - As the core support component of major equipment, the running state of rolling bearing is crucial for equipment health management. However, in actual industrial settings, equipment often operates under conditions of variable speeds and loads, resulting in significant differences in the distribution of training and test data. Meanwhile, the problem of scarce fault samples is widespread, making it difficult for traditional transfer learning methods to meet engineering requirements. To address the challenge of few-shot fault diagnosis while considering the correlation between the two domains, this study proposes a cross-scenarios few-shot fault diagnosis for rolling bearings via inter-domain similarity guided meta-learning with dual-attention multiscale feature denoising network (SMLDMN). The method introduces a inter-domain similarity-based sample selection strategy to select the most representative data samples from known domains by quantifying source-target domain similarity, improving classification accuracy. An adaptive denoising multiscale attention-enhanced feature extraction network is developed, combining dual attention mechanisms and multiscale convolutional modules to hierarchically extract time-frequency features from vibration signals, enhancing feature representation. Soft threshold denoising is further applied for adaptive noise reduction. Experiments on two bearing datasets validate the method's effectiveness in cross-domain few-shot fault identification under varying operational conditions and different equipment scenarios. Comparative results show consistent accuracy gains over some existing methods, along with strong generalization and stability, and the studied method can provide a viable solution for intelligent maintenance in fields such as wind power, and intelligent manufacturing.
