AB  - Closed-chain linkage mechanisms are widely used in the leg structure design of legged robots due to their advantages of single degree-of-freedom and high structural stiffness. To obtain closed-chain mobile robots that meet motion performance requirements, foot trajectory planning and dimensional synthesis are indispensable design elements. However, most current methods rely on empirical human judgment for foot trajectory planning, which lacks objectivity. Furthermore, the subsequent application of traditional dimensional synthesis methods also suffers from poor solution accuracy. To address these two issues, this study integrates deep reinforcement learning (DRL) algorithms to propose a novel and intelligent design method for closed-chain legged mechanisms. In terms of foot trajectory planning, this method leverages the reward-maximizing characteristics of DRL to directly obtain foot trajectory information that meets the desired motion performance by training a quadruped robot. The obtained trajectory further serves as a critical reference for the screening of closed-chain leg configurations and dimensional synthesis. Additionally, to enhance the reproducibility accuracy of the trajectory, this method combines DRL with dimensional synthesis, achieving precise adjustment of the leg mechanism dimensions through iterative learning by the agent. This paper takes the realization of fast, stable, and energy-efficient locomotion on flat ground as a research case and conducts relevant experimental validation. The experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method exhibits high feasibility and practicality for designing closed-chain legged mobile robots.
