AB  - This paper investigates the complexity of intelligent decision-making within multiagent systems operating under uncertainty, particularly emphasizing the challenges associated with modeling behaviors of other agents and optimizing decision-making for a subject agent in a common environment characterized by incomplete historical data. To address these challenges, we propose a generative learning method based on a general multiagent decision making framework, namely interactive dynamic influence diagrams, and apply contrastive learning to diversify the generation of potential behaviors, thereby enhancing the subject agent’s modeling and prediction capabilities. We conduct experiments on multiple classic domains to demonstrate the efficacy of the new learning method in improving decision-making quality. The empirical results highlight its substantial improvement in enhancing overall performance in multiagent decision-making. Our work contributes to multiagent decision making particularly when a subject agent interacts with other unknown agents, including humans, in many practical applications.
