AB  - Collision avoidance for unmanned surface vehicles (USV) using deep reinforcement learning (DRL) has been widely studied in recent years. However, the performance of such methods is often constrained by the need for manually crafted reward functions, which limits generalization in diverse maritime scenarios. To address this issue, a reward-free collision avoidance approach is proposed that integrates adversarial inverse reinforcement learning (AIRL) with a diffusion model. A diffusion model is first trained on expert navigation data and then employed for data augmentation, generating diverse and informative trajectories. These samples, together with the original data, are used to construct a hybrid dataset for training the AIRL framework, in which the policy generator is optimized via the soft actor–critic (SAC) algorithm. The proposed method is evaluated on the Imazu problem and in various randomly generated multi-ship environments, achieving better performance compared with AIRL, generative adversarial imitation learning, stable AIRL, an artificial potential field method, deep deterministic policy gradient and SAC. Furthermore, real-ship experiments are conducted to validate the effectiveness and robustness of the proposed algorithm in practical USV operations. This research provides a novel solution and theoretical foundation for the development of autonomous and intelligent decision-making systems for USV.
