AB  - Pathological voice classification is critical for early diagnosis and monitoring of voice disorders. However, practical deployment of deep learning models is limited by microphone mismatch, where recordings from different devices cause substantial performance degradation. This work introduces the Pathological Voice Classification with Domain Adversarial Training (PVCDAT) framework, a model-agnostic approach that extracts microphone-invariant features while preserving pathological discriminability. A microphone-diverse dataset was also generated for this study by convolving real pathological recordings with impulse responses from representative microphones, simulating realistic device variability. PVCDAT integrates a shared encoder, dual prediction branches, a gradient reversal layer, and a class-weighting mechanism to simultaneously address domain shift and class imbalance. Evaluations of three deep learning models show consistent improvements in unweighted average recall (up to 7.66% under mismatched conditions) and flexibility in applying PVCDAT. Principal component analysis further confirmed improved class separability. Therefore, PVCDAT offers a practical and scalable solution for robust pathological voice classification.
